    landscape: "The Unemployment Insurance Modernization and Recession Readiness Act is the most recent federal-state-level proposal for expansion of benefits. The reintroduced proposal (2025) is designed to raise the base of benefits when unemployment rises, minimize disparities among states by setting a federal standard, and create permanent benefits for gig workers.<br><br>Modernization has consistent bipartisan support in principle. The political fault line runs through coverage scope for gig workers and the financing base. New Jersey's S.1840 (2026) reflects growing state-level willingness to use the unemployment system as the displacement-response vehicle. State-level momentum is strongest in New Jersey, Washington, and Massachusetts.<br><br>Pandemic Unemployment Assistance (2020) demonstrated that coverage can be extended rapidly when political will exists. Several U.S. states have enacted independent expansions.<br><br>Washington State's expanded unemployment insurance coverage for gig workers offers a partial model; Massachusetts and New Jersey have enacted coverage extensions. The Pandemic Unemployment Assistance program showed that universal coverage is administratively achievable within weeks. The barriers are political, not logistical.",
